1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,320
Evidence-based practice combines research with clinical expertise and
the patient's values and circumstances. None of these inputs works
well in isolation.

2
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:23,256
A study may describe an average benefit, but your patient may have
different risks, resources or priorities. Clinical judgement asks
whether the evidence fits this person and the care you are authorized
to provide.

3
00:00:23,256 --> 00:00:33,456
Your experience helps you recognize patterns and implementation
problems. It does not make a memorable success stronger than a well-
conducted body of research.

4
00:00:33,456 --> 00:00:45,984
Likewise, a patient's preference matters even when it differs from the
option with the best average result. Explain the options, check
understanding and work within the applicable clinical guidance.

5
00:00:45,984 --> 00:00:56,208
Quantitative research uses measured data. A trial comparing the
frequency of missed handover information between two methods asks a
quantitative question.

6
00:00:56,208 --> 00:01:08,856
Qualitative research explores experiences and meanings, often through
interviews or observations. Asking why patients feel excluded during
handover is a different and equally useful question.

7
00:01:08,856 --> 00:01:23,688
A randomized trial can reduce confounding for an intervention
comparison. A cohort study follows groups without assigning treatment.
A case report can reveal a problem but cannot reliably establish how
often a treatment works.

8
00:01:23,688 --> 00:01:35,640
A systematic review brings studies together using explicit methods.
Its value still depends on the included studies and how the review was
performed, not simply its place on a hierarchy.

9
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:46,560
Knowledge translation connects research with actual practice. Findings
need appraisal, synthesis and interpretation before they become a safe
local process.

10
00:01:46,560 --> 00:02:00,840
Prehospital care has particular constraints: moving vehicles, limited
personnel, variable equipment and long distances to definitive care.
Results from a well-resourced hospital may not transfer directly.

11
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:12,480
Implementation includes the authorized protocol, team training,
equipment and monitoring for unintended effects. An article does not
itself add a procedure to your scope.

12
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:25,008
Report field barriers and audit results through the appropriate
channel. Good feedback explains where a process failed and what
resources were missing, rather than silently creating a private
protocol.

13
00:02:25,008 --> 00:02:35,928
PICO stands for population, intervention, comparison and outcome. It
turns a broad question such as are prompts useful into a question that
a study can answer.

14
00:02:35,928 --> 00:02:46,392
Our original example asks whether new paramedic students using a
structured handover prompt omit less critical information than
students using their usual approach.

15
00:02:46,392 --> 00:02:59,328
Specify outcomes before interpreting results. Fewer omissions may be
valuable, but a delay in urgent treatment would also matter. A single
convenient score may miss an important harm.

16
00:02:59,328 --> 00:03:09,768
When reading an abstract, write these four elements in your own words.
If the population or comparison is unclear, that uncertainty belongs
in the appraisal.

17
00:03:09,768 --> 00:03:19,656
Validity asks whether the estimate is trustworthy. Selection bias can
occur when the people included differ systematically from those who
should have been studied.

18
00:03:19,656 --> 00:03:31,272
Confounding occurs when another difference between groups helps
explain the outcome. If only experienced crews receive a new tool,
experience may partly explain a better result.

19
00:03:31,272 --> 00:03:42,456
Blinding can reduce influence from knowing the assigned intervention.
In a simulation study, a student may see the prompt, while an assessor
can still be blinded to the study group.

20
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:54,336
Relevance is a separate judgement. A rigorously conducted classroom
study can still leave uncertainty about noisy emergency scenes,
experienced crews and actual patient outcomes.

21
00:03:54,336 --> 00:04:04,440
An effect estimate describes the size of a difference. Its uncertainty
matters: a small study may leave a wide range of plausible effects.

22
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:18,024
A p-value is calculated under a specified statistical model, often a
no-difference model. It concerns obtaining results at least as extreme
as those observed, assuming that model and its assumptions.

23
00:04:18,024 --> 00:04:27,768
It is not the probability that the treatment works, or that the result
was caused by chance. A threshold alone cannot establish clinical
importance.

24
00:04:27,768 --> 00:04:39,648
Likewise, a result that is not statistically significant does not
prove that two approaches are equivalent. Look at design, effect
estimates and uncertainty before drawing a conclusion.

25
00:04:39,648 --> 00:04:51,240
These numbers are invented solely for appraisal practice; they are not
a published trial or evidence of clinical benefit. Thirty students in
each group perform one simulated handover.

26
00:04:51,240 --> 00:05:02,280
The prompt group has fewer mean omissions but takes longer. Random
allocation and a blinded assessor strengthen the design, provided they
were actually implemented as described.

27
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:12,888
Important questions remain: how was the checklist chosen, were groups
comparable, were any students excluded, and how uncertain is the
estimated difference?

28
00:05:12,888 --> 00:05:22,800
Write a conclusion that acknowledges both the possible benefit and the
limits. Do not turn a classroom omission score into a claim that
patient survival improves.

29
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:33,552
A cautious but useful conclusion is: the prompt may reduce omissions
in this student simulation, but the estimate is incomplete and the
longer handover needs attention.

30
00:05:33,552 --> 00:05:45,096
Ask for the full methods and uncertainty estimates before judging
strength. The study does not tell us whether the result persists,
generalizes to the field or benefits patients.

31
00:05:45,096 --> 00:05:57,288
For an educational improvement, a supervised pilot might be reasonable
after approval. Define what would count as a successful change and
watch for delays or mechanical checklist reading.

32
00:05:57,288 --> 00:06:09,624
For a clinical intervention, evidence review must also pass through
the appropriate governance, scope, training and protocol process.
Personal enthusiasm is not implementation authority.

33
00:06:09,624 --> 00:06:21,000
AET asks you to think about PARAMEDIC2. This randomized, double-blind
UK trial compared epinephrine with saline placebo during adult out-of-
hospital cardiac arrest.

34
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:35,952
Thirty-day survival was 3.2 percent versus 2.4 percent. Favourable
neurological outcome at discharge was 2.2 percent versus 1.9 percent,
without a statistically significant difference.

35
00:06:35,952 --> 00:07:01,488
At discharge, severe neurological impairment was reported in thirty-
nine of one hundred and twenty-six survivors given epinephrine, versus
sixteen of ninety placebo survivors. Those are survivor denominators,
not all randomized patients. Discuss this finding alongside the
separate survival and favourable-outcome comparisons; it does not
establish what caused every survivor's disability.

36
00:07:01,488 --> 00:07:12,384
Appraisal is also different from issuing a treatment order. Use the
applicable resuscitation protocol and your authorization; this lesson
does not change either.

37
00:07:12,384 --> 00:07:21,960
A practice gap is a difference between your current performance and an
appropriate standard. It is a starting point for learning, not a
verdict about your worth.

38
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:32,904
Internal triggers include hesitation or uncertainty. External triggers
include a preceptor's feedback, a documentation audit, a near miss or
a changed guideline.

39
00:07:32,904 --> 00:07:45,024
Describe the behaviour, its context and its consequence. I omitted
treatment response in two simulated handovers is actionable; I am bad
at communication is too broad.

40
00:07:45,024 --> 00:08:07,320
Compare your impression with observed feedback. The school uses the
Dunning-Kruger label to highlight that limited skill can also limit
recognition of mistakes. Treat it as a reason to seek calibration, not
a diagnosis of every confident novice. Confidence can rise without
skill improving; an uncertain student may still perform accurately.

41
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:17,856
SWOT separates internal strengths and weaknesses from external
opportunities and threats. It helps you plan around real conditions
instead of listing vague ambitions.

42
00:08:17,856 --> 00:08:29,904
A strength could be a consistent assessment sequence. A weakness could
be missing the latest observations during handover. An opportunity
could be access to a supervised simulation lab.

43
00:08:29,904 --> 00:08:41,088
A threat might be a shift pattern that prevents attendance at one lab.
Change the action plan by arranging another supervised session, rather
than lowering the safety standard.

44
00:08:41,088 --> 00:08:52,128
The downloaded AET teaching asks for at least two weaknesses and at
least one opportunity in the personal CPD document. Use genuine
observations from your own learning.

45
00:08:52,128 --> 00:09:01,248
DACA means describe, analyze, conclude and act. Start with a brief
factual account, not an exhaustive retelling of the call.

46
00:09:01,248 --> 00:09:15,120
In our fictional simulation, the student reported the first blood
pressure but omitted the newer reading. Analysis asks why: perhaps the
note layout hid the trend and rehearsal focused only on the initial
assessment.

47
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:26,352
The conclusion is a specific learning need: communicating the latest
status and meaningful change. The action should practise that exact
behaviour and include independent feedback.

48
00:09:26,352 --> 00:09:38,352
Keep identifiable patient details out of a personal learning
portfolio. Reflection needs enough context to explain the learning,
not enough detail to reconstruct a patient's identity.

49
00:09:38,352 --> 00:09:50,544
SMART stands for specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-
bound. A goal states the improvement you want to demonstrate; an
activity is how you plan to reach it.

50
00:09:50,544 --> 00:10:04,248
Read about handover is an activity, not a performance outcome. A
better goal is to deliver three varied simulated handovers with the
latest observations, trends and treatment response, checked by a
supervisor.

51
00:10:04,248 --> 00:10:15,384
Set a feasible date and a clear success criterion. The practice count
is an example for your plan, not an AET pass mark or proof of
independent clinical competence.

52
00:10:15,384 --> 00:10:27,336
If the goal depends on rare real patient opportunities, choose a
supervised simulation alternative. Do not perform an unnecessary
procedure merely to complete a learning target.

53
00:10:27,336 --> 00:10:36,792
A CPD plan connects a gap to a goal, actions, evidence, dates and
review. Each part should support the same learning need.

54
00:10:36,792 --> 00:10:48,984
Select a resource because it addresses the gap. For a local handover
process, the applicable service guideline is more directly relevant
than a general article about communication.

55
00:10:48,984 --> 00:10:58,920
Plan both learning and performance. Reading explains the framework;
rehearsal and observed feedback show whether you can use it under
realistic conditions.

56
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:09,792
Document a review date before starting. If the result is not achieved,
change the learning action or obtain further support, and record the
remaining gap honestly.

57
00:11:09,792 --> 00:11:21,144
This is an original example, not a completed answer to the school's
capstone. The performance gap is omission of a clinically important
change, not simply a lack of confidence.

58
00:11:21,144 --> 00:11:32,736
The learner reviews the applicable handover guideline, changes the
note layout to show times and trends, and practises three different
scenarios with a supervisor before the next skills session.

59
00:11:32,736 --> 00:11:44,496
The supervisor records whether the latest findings, major change and
treatment response were communicated. The learner keeps a de-
identified checklist and a short DACA reflection.

60
00:11:44,496 --> 00:11:54,576
A later repeat checks retention. If the learner still reads only the
first measurements, the gap remains open even though the reading and
practice sessions were completed.

61
00:11:54,576 --> 00:12:10,968
Formal activities include structured courses and workshops. Informal
development can include self-directed reading, peer discussion and
reflection. Both can support learning, but credit eligibility depends
on the applicable regulatory rules.

62
00:12:10,968 --> 00:12:33,408
The school's six-part reflective entry asks what happened, what you
learned, why it matters, what action you took, what evidence you kept
and when you will review it. Add the activity and date, organize by
topic and retain the supporting record. Separate a planned action from
a completed one; a tidy folder does not itself prove improvement.

63
00:12:33,408 --> 00:12:46,008
A personal portfolio is not a copy of the patient chart. Remove direct
identifiers and combinations that could identify a person, especially
in a small community or an unusual event.

64
00:12:46,008 --> 00:12:54,960
Use approved secure systems for any official patient-contact
reporting. A private teaching example should contain no identifiable
clinical record.

65
00:12:54,960 --> 00:13:05,616
A weak entry records only attendance. A stronger entry explains the
learning need, the learning itself and how it influenced a decision or
performance.

66
00:13:05,616 --> 00:13:18,024
For example: I learned to pair each intervention with a timed
response; my supervisor observed that all three rehearsed handovers
included this information; I will repeat the task next week.

67
00:13:18,024 --> 00:13:29,112
Keep the actual evidence where permitted. Do not claim an audit proved
improvement if no comparison was made, and do not write a planned
action as though it has already occurred.

68
00:13:29,112 --> 00:13:39,024
A useful portfolio can show an unfinished gap honestly. That makes it
possible to obtain further learning rather than disguising attendance
as mastery.

69
00:13:39,024 --> 00:13:48,912
Review a CPD plan after meaningful feedback and at scheduled
intervals. Successful practice may close one gap while revealing
another.

70
00:13:48,912 --> 00:14:00,960
Keep school completion, service training and regulatory continuing
competence distinct. One activity may support more than one purpose,
but automatic credit should not be assumed.

71
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:14,448
The current BC regulation describes continuing education, patient
contacts, reporting and supporting records, with defined exceptions.
Check the requirements applicable to your licence and reporting
period.

72
00:14:14,448 --> 00:14:27,408
For this week, the immediate learning task is a specific, defensible
personal development plan. A well-organized portfolio supports that
task, but organization alone does not prove clinical competence.

73
00:14:27,408 --> 00:14:37,728
The downloaded teaching asks you to build a personal CPD document and
complete a capstone using a school-provided vignette and template.
Your work must be your own.

74
00:14:37,728 --> 00:15:02,040
The saved instructions require at least one SMART, patient-centred
goal and at least one relevant evidence source that you appraise and
justify. The criteria connect a specific learning need, a measurable
improvement action, evidence-based relevance and complete
documentation. Explain those connections using your own work, not an
invented completed experience.

75
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:11,616
A blank planning aid is included with this lecture. It is not the
official school template, a submitted assignment or a model answer to
the school's vignette.

76
00:15:11,616 --> 00:15:21,648
Before submission, confirm the current school instructions and form.
This course does not open assessments, submit work or mark an LMS task
complete.
